<html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Technology and young people&#x27;s moods</title><!--[if gte mso 9]><xml><w:WordDocument><w:View>Print</w:View></w:WordDocument></xml><![endif]--><style>body,p,li,td{font-family:Cambria,Georgia,serif;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.35;color:#17130f}h1,h2,h3{font-family:Cambria,Georgia,serif;font-weight:normal;color:#8c1912}h1{font-size:20pt}h2{font-size:14pt}p.note{font-size:9pt}td,th{border:1px solid #e4dcd0;padding:3pt 5pt;font-size:9pt;vertical-align:top}table{border-collapse:collapse}.pb{page-break-before:always}</style></head><body><p style="color:#8b7f72;font-size:9pt">RUBICON REPORT</p><h1>Technology and young people&#x27;s moods</h1><p style="color:#8b7f72;font-size:9pt">Run 2026-10-20-wxbh</p><p class="asked"><b>Question</b> In the 12 pilot interviews I04, I06, I07, I17, I18, I24, I28, I29, I37, I42, I43 and I48 (drawn at random within each borough and gender from the 48 interviews with young Londoners about loneliness), what different roles do the young people describe technology and social media playing in their own positive and negative moods, how many people describe each role, and on which side of mood?

The case is one person (one interview); a person can describe several roles, on both sides. A passage counts where the speaker ties a technology to a feeling of their own, positive or negative, including feeling lonely or feeling connected. Technology covers social media, messaging, phone and video calls, gaming, streaming and the internet generally. Any role counts: starting a feeling, keeping it going, easing it or lifting it. Feelings the interviewer suggests and the speaker does not take up, feelings of other people, and general remarks about social media or &quot;people these days&quot; that the speaker does not apply to themselves do not count. Accounts the interviewer prompted by raising technology count, but are marked, so the answer can say how many raised a role unprompted.

The roles are drawn from the interviews: each passage is coded openly in the speaker&#x27;s words, the labels are gathered into a short list of roles (a soft recode of the open coding, each passage read once), and the evaluator sees the list with an example passage for each before anything is counted by it. Every passage of the 12 interviews is read in full. The answer describes the roles, counted by person, with quotations, and gives no verdict. A fresh reader checks the answer against the passages it cites and the documents before it is shown (a check, not a blind recode).

Separately from the answer, report anything in the documents that bears on the question but does not fit the frame it sets, each point once, in its own section, which the rest of the answer points to rather than repeats.</p><table style="border-collapse:collapse;margin:8pt 0 12pt;width:100%"><tr><td style="border:1px solid #d9b9b2;border-left:4pt solid #8c1912;background:#fdfbf6;padding:6pt 10pt"><p style="margin:0;font-family:Cambria,Georgia,serif;font-size:13pt;color:#8c1912">What was automatically checked in this report</p><p style="margin:0 0 4pt;font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:8.5pt;color:#7d7166">Checked when this report was created</p><p style="margin:3pt 0 3pt 14pt;text-indent:-14pt;font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10pt"><span style="color:#8c1912;font-weight:bold">&#10003;</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>44</b> numbers, every one computed directly from the coded passages rather than written by the AI.</p><p style="margin:3pt 0 3pt 14pt;text-indent:-14pt;font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10pt"><span style="color:#8c1912;font-weight:bold">&#10003;</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>100</b> quotations, every one found word for word in its interview.</p><p style="margin:3pt 0 3pt 14pt;text-indent:-14pt;font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10pt"><span style="color:#8c1912;font-weight:bold">&#10003;</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>3</b> key claims tagged as numbers are, each with the passages for it and against it and why they bear it out.</p><p style="margin:3pt 0 3pt 14pt;text-indent:-14pt;font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10pt"><span style="color:#8c1912;font-weight:bold">&#10003;</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;Other numbers in the text, in grey, are the writer's own, such as a year or a number quoted from a source, and were not counted by code.</p><p style="margin:3pt 0 3pt 14pt;text-indent:-14pt;font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10pt"><span style="color:#8c1912;font-weight:bold">&#10003;</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>73</b> sentences of the answer were read against the interviews by a second AI reader, separate from the one that wrote them.</p><p style="margin:3pt 0 3pt 14pt;text-indent:-14pt;font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10pt"><span style="color:#7d7166;font-weight:bold">&#8211;</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Not checked:</b> whether the passages cited are all the documents hold on each point, or whether each is labelled correctly.</p></td></tr></table><h2 id="s-the-answer">The answer</h2>
<p>Across the 12 interviews, the young people describe the following roles that technology plays in their own moods, counted by person.</p>
<p>Lifting or easing mood:</p>
<ul><li>Keeping in touch with particular people by call, message, social media or a shared series (6 of 12; without prompting, 6 of 12).</li><li>Entertainment and distraction: YouTube, Netflix, phone and telly, console games, funny posts and music (6 of 12).</li><li>Contact on their own terms, choosing when to talk or to log off (2 of 12).</li><li>Comfort when lonely or low, mostly from music (2 of 12).</li><li>Recognition from posting and motivation from pictures (1 of 12, I06).</li></ul>
<p>Lowering mood:</p>
<ul><li>Comparing themselves with others&#x27; lives, looks or luxury on screen (5 of 12).</li><li>Screens that fail to fill empty time or leave them still alone (5 of 12).</li><li>Fear of judgement keeping them off social media, or technology as the means of withdrawing (3 of 12).</li><li>Seeing in posts that they were left out (2 of 12).</li><li>Messages and calls that let them down (2 of 12).</li><li>Phones and games coming between them and the people they are with (2 of 12).</li><li>Dismay at social media&#x27;s fakery (2 of 12).</li><li>Being cut off without a phone (1 of 12, I24).</li></ul>
<p>Most describe technology on both sides of their mood: 9 of 12 people tie it to a feeling of their own that it lifts and to one that it lowers. 1 of 12 people (I29) describe it only lifting their mood, 1 of 12 (I48) only lowering it, and 1 of 12 (I18) never tie it to a feeling of their own.</p>
<p>Two patterns run through these roles. Contact with a particular person through a screen is told mostly as easing loneliness, though I04 and I42 describe messages and calls that hurt, while watching or playing alone is told both ways, as failing to fill the gap and as filling time or lifting mood (I28, “Um well, I wouldn’t say…”; I29, “Whereas- like for example, once…”; I17, “Alright, I feel like. ‘Cause…”; I28, “Yes, it’s not physical interaction…”; I24, “Like me, I will enjoy…”; I24, “Yeah, when I’m at home…”; I43, “you start seeing the person…”; I28, “It’s literally just me in…”; I48, “So, as long as I’m…”; I48, “I put home, because [laughing]…”; I43, “Because sometimes you might watch…”; I17, “Like, you can watch telly…”; I28, “Um well, I wouldn’t say…”; I42, “I like going on YouTube…”; I42, “your feeling of loneliness can…”; I17, “Because sometimes I do like…”). Most of the negative accounts of social media are about the measure it holds up: others&#x27; better lives, and events the speaker was left out of; the others are fear of being judged (I28), dismay at its fakery (I04, I42) and browsing that brings mood down (I43) (I06, “So, I think there is…”; I06, “I think that&#x27;s what the…”; I07, “but there are negatives to…”; I07, “that can make you like…”; I07, “it will be like a…”; I17, “Well if you’re sad and…”; I17, “Yeah, sometimes it does make…”; I43, “So, I feel like everything…”; I43, “It’s a bit of jealousy…”; I06, “but with a lot of…”; I06, “so when you… when you…”; I06, “sometimes when I&#x27;m at, when…”; I06, “at first you might view…”; I06, “and then you might think…”; I42, “I noticed on a, just…”).</p>

<h2 id="s-what-lifts-mood">What lifts mood</h2>
<p>10 of 12 people describe technology lifting or easing their mood. Keeping in touch is the role raised most often without prompting (6 of 12). A call makes being alone in their apartment less bad for one interviewee, though it is still the loneliest place: &quot;it’s not as bad as living in Essex, because even when I’m in my apartment I have some things to do, like whether I’m on the phone with a friend&quot; (I28, “Um well, I wouldn’t say…”). In their first year at university, on a doctor&#x27;s advice, the same interviewee called home, and &quot;it did help a little bit, not too much&quot; (I28, “Yes, it’s not physical interaction…”). Another recalls friends from university and sixth form who &quot;lit you up when you felt so down or lonely, just a phone call away&quot; (I29, “Whereas- like for example, once…”), and adds that &quot;since I started working, you know, it’s not the same as before&quot; (I29). Others call or message someone when bored of their own company (I24, “Like me, I will enjoy…”), know that they &quot;could call one of my friends&quot; (I17, “Alright, I feel like. ‘Cause…”), or had family texts checking on them during exams (I07, “like during exam season like…”).</p>
<p>Entertainment is mostly screens used alone: one interviewee likes &quot;going on YouTube and just watching stuff that I never think to learn&quot; (I42, “I like going on YouTube…”), and another likes to &quot;sit there and go on my phone and watch telly&quot; (I17, “Because sometimes I do like…”). Comfort is narrower: turning to music when lonely or low, to share and understand the feeling, in I06 (I06, “so I think that for…”; I06, “Yeah, and I feel like…”), and to motivational YouTube videos, &quot;whatever helps&quot;, in I28, in answer to a question that raised social media (I28, “With everything [unintelligible 32:58] internet…”).</p>
<p>For contact on their own terms (2 of 12), the value is control. One interviewee says &quot;being in bed with my phone it&#x27;s like I can be myself and still be accepted&quot; and &quot;I can just log off, you know. When you&#x27;re with people you cannot just walk away.&quot; (I37, “It could be anywhere but…”; I37, “So if i feel like…”). Another is &quot;quite careful with how I manage my social media, who I follow, who I talk to, so it&#x27;s not an unpleasant feeling when I&#x27;m on social media, it&#x27;s normally good&quot; (I07, “but generally speaking I&#x27;m quite…”). Recognition from posting Snapchat stories, and motivation from gym pictures, appear only in I06 (I06, “So that kind of, that…”; I06, “and I think with certain…”); the same interviewee&#x27;s second thoughts about posting are under Outside the frame.</p>
<h2 id="s-what-lowers-mood">What lowers mood</h2>
<p>10 of 12 people describe technology starting, keeping going or worsening a bad feeling of their own. Comparison (5 of 12) is mostly about social media. Seeing others &quot;all happy&quot; on social media when already sad and lonely makes one interviewee feel worse: &quot;Not worse, but it does a little bit&quot; (I17, “Well if you’re sad and…”). Another feels that people on Instagram are better: &quot;I just almighty feel like they&#x27;re better than me&quot; (I07, “it will be like a…”). A third &quot;sometimes does get a bit jealousy&quot; at luxury lives on Instagram and Facebook, and at once discounts it (I43, “So, I feel like everything…”). Outside social media, one penalises themselves each morning for not looking like &quot;the models on TV&quot; (I37, “It could be getting up…”).</p>
<p>Seeing that they were left out is close to comparison but about a particular event: photos of outings, when &quot;you start feeling alone&quot; (I06, “so when you… when you…”), or learning, on Facebook as far as they remember, that friends went out without them (I42, “I noticed on a, just…”).</p>
<p>Empty filler is the commonest negative role outside social media: &quot;Sometimes, TV can be boring to watch, so you just turn it off&quot; (I48, “So, as long as I’m…”). Of their first year at university, another says of watching YouTube alone: &quot;But at the end of the day, you know, I’m still alone.&quot; (I28, “It’s literally just me in…”).</p>
<p>Fear of judgement and withdrawal keep loneliness going (3 of 12). One interviewee keeps off Snapchat and Instagram for fear of being judged, which leaves them &quot;forced to sort of keep myself to myself&quot; (I28, “It- if- in a way…”; I28, “And they would want an…”), and &quot;that kind of sort of made me feel more lonely&quot; (I28). Another texts excuses to stay in bed because &quot;you’re scared of their opinion&quot; (I37, “So final box is just…”). A third used to doubt whether people would like their true self behind the character they had built on social media (I42, “Loneliness is in, I, when…”).</p>
<p>Messages and calls let people down: the phone going off after a breakup and not being the ex (I04, “and then your phone goes…”); &quot;looking at your phone, waiting for an invitation&quot; that &quot;just doesn&#x27;t come through&quot; (I42, “I kind of thought was…”), which the same interviewee then qualifies (see Outside the frame); and friends lying by message, and check-up calls that &quot;kinda of got depressing&quot; (I42, “I remembered going and seeing…”; I42, “it was like people were…”).</p>
<p>Phones and games come between people in company: with everyone on their phones at a meal, &quot;it kind of takes that connection away&quot; (I43, “Sometimes it does get annoying…”), and with friends playing FIFA, a game the interviewee dislikes, &quot;you kind of have to disconnect with people&quot; (I42, “Okay, so two friends are…”). Dismay at social media&#x27;s fakery is &quot;one of despair because it’s not gonna change&quot; (I42, “So yeah, I don’t think…”). Being without a phone makes one interviewee &quot;feel so lost&quot; (I24, “But this links a lot…”). One passage fits none of these roles: reading online about their symptoms &quot;was just scaring me like all the time&quot; (I28, “And then I started reading…”).</p>
<h2 id="s-alone-or-with-someone">Alone or with someone</h2>
<p>In I43, watching a Netflix series &quot;no one watches&quot; leaves &quot;no one to talk to&quot;, while finding &quot;the person that watches the same series&quot; means &quot;it kind of fills that loneliness&quot; (I43, “Because sometimes you might watch…”; I43, “you start seeing the person…”). Screens used alone leave people still alone or bored in I17, I28, I43 and I48 (I17, “Like, you can watch telly…”; I28, “It’s literally just me in…”; I43, “Because sometimes you might watch…”; I48, “I put home, because [laughing]…”), but fill time or lift mood in I17, I28 and I42 (I17, “Because sometimes I do like…”; I28, “Um well, I wouldn’t say…”; I42, “your feeling of loneliness can…”); I28 tells both, YouTube alone in their first year and Netflix on the laptop in their apartment now. In I37 the control a phone gives over contact is both a relief and the means of the excuses that keep them in bed (I37, “So if i feel like…”; I37, “So final box is just…”; I37, “It could be anywhere but…”; I37, “So if i feel like…”; I37, “But when you&#x27;re home you…”; I37, “It does make me feel…”; I37, “I can just be who…”; I37, “So final box is just…”; I07, “but generally speaking I&#x27;m quite…”); being without a phone, by contrast, is told as loss (I24, “It’s like people get addicted…”).</p>
<h2 id="s-by-kind-of-technology">By kind of technology</h2>
<p>Social media is tied to negative feelings by 7 of 12 people and to positive ones by 4 of 12. Messaging and calls run the other way: 6 of 12 positive against 4 of 12 negative. Gaming and streaming fall on both sides (5 of 12 positive, 5 of 12 negative). No passage was told as helping and harming at once in a way that could not be split (0 of 12).</p>

<h2 id="s-outside-the-frame">Outside the frame</h2>
<p>Some of the strongest views about technology in these interviews are about other people, not the speaker&#x27;s own mood, and so were not counted: 9 of 12 people give such general views. One links social media comparison to loneliness among young people in general: &quot;that leads them to be lonely&quot; (I29, “Cause you’re looking into a…”); another sees social media as &quot;just like drugs&quot; for some people (I04, “I think social media is…”).</p>
<p>There are doubts inside some accounts (6 of 12). Asked how they feel about people on social media showing a different image from what they feel inside, one interviewee says &quot;I don’t really feel anything about it&quot; (I17, “Um… Mm, I don’t really…”), shortly after saying such posts make them feel &quot;a little bit&quot; worse (I17, “Well if you’re sad and…”); of sitting in front of the telly for hours, the same interviewee says &quot;I don’t do it personally&quot; (I17, “Well, I don’t do it…”). Of their own Snapchat posts, another reflects that &quot;I could just enjoy their own company&quot; (I06), and that &quot;I felt the need to um for others to see and compare themselves with to make myself feel better&quot; (I06, “but I felt the need…”). Straight after describing waiting by the phone for an invitation, another says that as an introvert &quot;it’s a lot harder for me to kind of feel loneliness&quot; (I42, “I’m more of an introvert…”). One says that seeing what others have is &quot;never a negative feeling&quot;, before describing Instagram making them feel not good enough (I07, “so, there&#x27;s always that feeling…”). One, asked how being left out without a phone makes them feel, names doubting whether anyone likes them and then says &quot;But not in this case&quot; (I24, “Makes me feel unwanted. Um……”). And one gives friends a cover story for not being on social media (I28, “But what I used to…”).</p>
<p>Some interviewees reject or reframe the interviewer&#x27;s suggestion (2 of 12). Asked whether friends gaming left them feeling left out, one says &quot;it wasn’t more a feeling left out&quot; (I42, “Um, it wasn’t more a…”); another says that going round in circles at home is &quot;not just a phone situation&quot; (I17, “Yeah, with that and with…”). One describes chatting on their phone as &quot;a massive time-waster&quot; that kept them up late, with no feeling attached (I04, “I missed 45 minutes of…”), the one effect of this kind (1 of 12).</p>
<h2 id="s-limitations">Limitations</h2>
<p>This is a pilot of 12 interviews, so most roles rest on a few people, and several on one person. The interviews were about loneliness, not technology, and interviewers sometimes raised social media themselves: Comparison drops from 5 of 12 to 4 of 12 people when prompted accounts are set aside, and Fakery from 2 of 12 to 0 of 12. Each row records what a speaker says technology does to their own mood: a store of evidence about how it works, not a model of it (Powell, Copestake and Remnant 2024), and no measure of how much it does. One coder placed every passage, and a single reader checked the answer.</p>
<ul><li>Entertainment and Keeping in touch as the joint commonest positive roles (6 of 12 each) rest on counting music where no device is named (I06, I24) as technology; read the other way, Entertainment falls below Keeping in touch, which becomes the single commonest role, and Comfort rests on I28 alone.</li><li>That I29 is the one person for whom technology only lifts mood (1 of 12) rests on not reading as a role their remark that since starting work &quot;Whenever you message your friends, they’re busy, they can’t reply back&quot;, which they follow, when asked, with &quot;It’s really sad&quot; (I29); read as a negative role, nobody describes technology only lifting their mood.</li><li>Comparison&#x27;s unprompted count (4 of 12) rests on how follow-up questions are marked: I17&#x27;s account is marked unprompted because they raised social media first, though the interviewer&#x27;s question named it, while I07&#x27;s answers to questions about social media, which they had written on their sheet, are marked prompted; marked alike either way, the unprompted count would move by one person, down or up.</li></ul><h1 class="pb">Annexes</h1><h2>How this report was written</h2><p><b>What was read.</b> Rubicon read 12 of the 48 documents in full, chosen by name. The counts below are of people, one document each.</p><p><b>Coding.</b> It then marked every passage that bears on the question and recorded which side of the respondent&#x27;s mood the technology bears on in this passage, which kind of technology the passage is about, whether the interviewer raised technology or social media in the question this passage answers, so the respondent did not bring it up themselves and what the technology does to or for the respondent&#x27;s own feeling in this passage, as one specific thing with its direction, in close to the respondent&#x27;s own words, such as &#x27;seeing friends&#x27; posts makes me feel left out&#x27; or &#x27;video calls with family keep me feeling close&#x27;. It also marked every passage that bears on the question and recorded why the passage bears on the question but falls outside its frame and one sentence on what the passage shows and why it falls outside the frame. It grouped the answers of role label into themes, which became a codebook, and placed each passage under one of them.</p><p><b>Checking.</b> A separate reader then read the answer against the passages it cites and the documents, and checked every sentence and quotation, so the coding and the answer in this report are the corrected ones, counted again after the check.</p><p><b>Counting.</b> Code brought together, for each document, everything coded in any of its passages and documents, and recorded whether any passage was coded mood positive and whether any passage was coded mood negative. People were then counted in 6 tables, compared by these codings. The annex below gives each.</p><p><b>Claims.</b> The writer tagged 3 key claims that rest on no count, as each number is tagged: each names the passages for it and against it, how they would be found again and why they bear it out, and code checked that every passage named is one the coding holds.</p><p><b>Writing and checks.</b> Rubicon wrote the answer from these counts, citing for each finding the passages it rests on. Every number in the answer, 44 in all, was computed directly from the coded passages rather than written by the AI, and all 100 coded passages were found word for word in their documents; every quotation in the answer, 48 in all, was found in the documents.</p><div class="method" id="method"><h3>Step by step</h3><p>The analysis was written down as a workflow before it was counted: what to look for, how to code it, what to count. It was then counted directly from the coded passages rather than by the AI, so anyone can check a number, and another coder can follow the same definitions and code the interviews afresh.</p>
<h4>What was read</h4><p>All 48 documents, read in full: 24 male, 24 female.</p>
<section class="rb-block" id="s-howdone-s12" data-node="step:s12"><h4>Sampling: S12</h4></section>
<section class="rb-block" id="s-howdone-c-tech" data-node="step:c_tech"><h4>Coding: Tech</h4><p class="small">one row for each passage that fits; 78 rows.</p><details><summary>The instruction a second coder would follow</summary><blockquote>Read the interview transcript. Interviewer turns are marked &quot;I:&quot; or &quot;Interviewer:&quot; and the respondent&#x27;s &quot;R:&quot;, &quot;Interviewee:&quot; or &quot;Respondent:&quot;. Find every passage where the respondent ties a technology to a feeling of their own, positive or negative, including feeling lonely, left out, connected or supported. Technology covers social media (Instagram, Snapchat, TikTok, Facebook, Twitter, YouTube as a platform people post on), messaging apps, phone and video calls, gaming, streaming and watching video, and the internet or phone in general. Any role counts: the technology starting the feeling, keeping it going, easing it or lifting it. Give one row per distinct role in a passage; where one passage ties technology to two different feelings or in two different ways, give two rows. Quote the respondent&#x27;s own words exactly, enough to show both the technology and the feeling, and never the interviewer&#x27;s words. Do not code: feelings the interviewer suggests that the respondent does not take up; feelings the respondent attributes to other people; general remarks about social media, phones or &quot;people these days&quot; that the respondent does not apply to their own feelings; mentions of technology with no feeling of the respondent&#x27;s attached; purely hypothetical feelings.</blockquote></details><h5>Role label</h5><p>What the technology does to or for the respondent&#x27;s own feeling in this passage, as one specific thing with its direction, in close to the respondent&#x27;s own words, such as &#x27;seeing friends&#x27; posts makes me feel left out&#x27; or &#x27;video calls with family keep me feeling close&#x27;. Name the role, not just the technology or the feeling.</p><h5>Mood</h5><p>Which side of the respondent&#x27;s mood the technology bears on in this passage.</p><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Positive</th><td>The technology lifts or eases the respondent&#x27;s mood: they feel better, connected, supported, entertained, calmer or less lonely because of it.</td><td>Feeling less lonely through messaging; enjoying gaming with friends; comfort from videos.</td><td>A passage where the same use also leaves them feeling worse goes to mixed.</td></tr><tr><th>Negative</th><td>The technology starts, keeps going or worsens a negative feeling of the respondent&#x27;s: lonely, left out, inadequate, anxious, low, isolated, envious.</td><td>Feeling left out seeing posts; feeling lonelier after scrolling; anxiety about replies.</td><td>Technology that eases a negative feeling goes to positive.</td></tr><tr><th>Mixed</th><td>The respondent says in the same passage that the same use both helps and harms their mood, or that it helps in one way and hurts in another, and the two cannot be split into separate rows.</td><td>&#x27;It keeps me in touch but it also makes me feel more alone.&#x27; when told as one inseparable point.</td><td>Where the two sides are separable statements, code two rows, one positive and one negative.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h5>Medium</h5><p>Which kind of technology the passage is about.</p><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Social media</th><td>Platforms where people post and see others&#x27; posts: Instagram, Snapchat stories, TikTok, Facebook, Twitter, YouTube as a feed.</td><td>Seeing posts, likes, followers, scrolling a feed.</td><td>Private one-to-one or group messages go to messaging_calls, even on the same app.</td></tr><tr><th>Messaging calls</th><td>Direct contact with particular people: texting, WhatsApp, direct messages, group chats, phone calls, FaceTime or video calls.</td><td>Texting a friend, calling family abroad, a group chat.</td><td>Public posts go to social_media.</td></tr><tr><th>Gaming streaming</th><td>Playing games (alone or online with others), watching TV, films, Netflix or YouTube videos for entertainment, listening to music on a device.</td><td>Online gaming with friends, binge-watching, music.</td><td>Posting or reading a feed goes to social_media.</td></tr><tr><th>Other tech</th><td>The phone or internet in general, or a technology that fits none of the above, such as online dating or online learning.</td><td>&#x27;Being on my phone all the time&#x27;, the internet generally, dating apps.</td><td>Anything that fits one of the three kinds above.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h5>Prompted</h5><p>yes where the interviewer raised technology or social media in the question this passage answers (or in the immediately preceding exchange), so the respondent did not bring it up themselves; no where the respondent brought technology up unprompted.</p></section>
<section class="rb-block" id="s-howdone-c-off" data-node="step:c_off"><h4>Coding: Off</h4><p class="small">one row for each passage that fits; 22 rows.</p><details><summary>The instruction a second coder would follow</summary><blockquote>Read the interview transcript. Find passages that bear on how technology and social media relate to the respondent&#x27;s own mood but that fall outside a coding of the respondent&#x27;s own feelings tied to technology: general views about technology and other people, doubts or contradictions in the respondent&#x27;s own account of technology and mood, suggestions by the interviewer about technology and feelings that the respondent rejects, and effects of technology on something other than mood. Quote the respondent&#x27;s own words exactly.</blockquote></details><h5>Kind</h5><p>Why the passage bears on the question but falls outside its frame.</p><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>General view</th><td>A view about what technology does to people in general, which the respondent does not apply to their own feelings in that passage.</td><td>&#x27;Girls aspire to look a certain way&#x27;; &#x27;we&#x27;re all glued to our phones&#x27;.</td><td>A general remark the respondent applies to their own feeling in the same passage is coded as a role, not here.</td></tr><tr><th>Doubt</th><td>Something in the respondent&#x27;s own account that cuts against or qualifies a role they describe elsewhere, such as saying they feel nothing about something they earlier said made them feel worse.</td><td>Contradictions, second thoughts, cover stories.</td><td>A plain change of topic.</td></tr><tr><th>Rejected suggestion</th><td>The interviewer suggests a feeling or role of technology and the respondent declines or reframes it.</td><td>&#x27;It wasn&#x27;t more a feeling left out&#x27;.</td><td>Minimal assent, which is neither coded here nor as a role.</td></tr><tr><th>Other effect</th><td>An effect of technology on the respondent&#x27;s time, sleep, work or relationships, with no feeling of theirs attached.</td><td>Losing sleep to messaging.</td><td>An effect with a stated feeling, which is a role.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h5>Point</h5><p>One sentence on what the passage shows and why it falls outside the frame.</p></section>
<section class="rb-block" id="s-howdone-g-roles" data-node="step:g_roles"><h4>Grouping: Roles</h4><p class="small">The role label labels of Coding: Tech, 78 distinct labels on 78 passages, drawn into 13 items, and each passage given the item its label is listed under.</p><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th><th>Open labels it gathers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Comparison<div class="small">Comparing myself with others&#x27; lives, looks or luxury online (negative)</div></th><td>Seeing other people&#x27;s apparently happier, better-looking or richer lives on screen makes the speaker feel worse about themselves: inferior, jealous, sad, not good enough.</td><td>Instagram lifestyles, bodies, bloggers&#x27; luxury, friends looking happy, models on TV.</td><td>Seeing friends at an event the speaker was not invited to (goes to Left out); Despair at social media&#x27;s fakery as such, not at the speaker&#x27;s own standing (goes to Fakery)</td><td>being bombarded with body and summer-body pictures overwhelms me and makes me lose my own goals, comparing myself to friends on my phone, then to people in the park, makes me feel worse and lonelier, fake portrayals of reality on Instagram distort the way I see myself and my goals, lifestyles portrayed on Instagram make me second-guess what I have and feel inferior, not good enough, seeing what others have makes me feel not pretty enough, upset, angry and a bit hopeless, seeing other people looking happy on social media when already sad and lonely makes me feel a little worse, seeing friends looking happy all the time on social media when alone makes me a bit sad, wishing my life were as easy, comparing myself to models on TV makes me penalise myself every morning, seeing people&#x27;s luxury lives on Instagram and Facebook sometimes makes me a bit jealous, seeing bloggers famous for nothing living in luxury makes me a bit jealous</td></tr><tr><th>Left out<div class="small">Seeing that I am left out (negative)</div></th><td>Posts or pictures show the speaker what others are doing without them, which leaves them feeling excluded, jealous, paranoid or lonely.</td><td>Photos of outings they were not at, checking where friends are, reading a negative story into a picture.</td><td>General envy of others&#x27; lives with no event the speaker was missing (goes to Comparison); Waiting for a message or invitation that does not come (goes to Let down by messages)</td><td>seeing on social media that I was not invited makes me feel jealous and mixed emotions, seeing fun photos of outings I am not in, alone on my bed scrolling, makes me feel alone, and I can&#x27;t escape them, seeing only the highlights on social media fools me and makes me feel even more lonely, viewing stuff on social media makes me paranoid, wondering if I did something wrong, a Snapchat picture of something I was not at makes me jealous, social media pictures let us feel lonely and blame others: when lonely I read the most negative story into a picture, checking social media in the park to see where my friends are without me sets off a chain of questions that leads to loneliness, seeing on Facebook that the lads went out without me left me feeling excluded and let down</td></tr><tr><th>Let down by messages<div class="small">Waiting for, or hurt by, messages and calls (negative)</div></th><td>Direct contact by phone or message disappoints or weighs on the speaker: a text that does not come, an invitation that never arrives, friends lying by message, or check-up calls that feel oppressive.</td><td>Waiting on the phone, lies by message, unwanted check-ups.</td><td>Seeing in a post that others went out (goes to Left out)</td><td>checking the phone after a breakup, hoping the text is from the ex and it is not, keeps the post-breakup loneliness and anticipation going, sitting at home looking at my phone waiting for an invitation that never comes makes me feel lonely, friends lying to me over messages about staying in made me feel lonely and hurt, friends calling and texting to check up on me while I withdrew got depressing</td></tr><tr><th>Hiding<div class="small">Fear of judgement keeps me off it, or it lets me hide (negative)</div></th><td>Fear of being judged online keeps the speaker from posting or from social media altogether, or technology becomes the means of withdrawing, and this keeps them lonely or doubting themselves.</td><td>Not taking selfies, staying off Snapchat, building a false character, texting excuses to avoid invitations.</td><td>Choosing when to be in contact, told as a relief (goes to On my own terms)</td><td>fear of being judged stops me taking selfies or sharing what I do, fear of being judged on Snapchat/Instagram keeps me off them, staying off social media for fear of hostile comments leaves me forced to keep to myself and lonelier, texting excuses to turn down invitations, out of fear of others&#x27; opinion, keeps me in my lonely bubble, building a character on social media used to make me doubt whether people would like my true self</td></tr><tr><th>Screens between people<div class="small">Screens come between me and the people I am with (negative)</div></th><td>When the speaker is with others, phones or games take attention away from them, so the speaker feels disconnected while in company.</td><td>Everyone on their phones at a meal, browsing Instagram while out, friends absorbed in a game the speaker dislikes.</td><td>Screens failing to fill time when alone (goes to Empty filler)</td><td>friends playing FIFA, which I dislike, makes me disconnect and wonder if they want me there, everyone, me included, being on our phones when out together is annoying and takes the connection away, when I&#x27;m lonely I end up checking my phone and browsing Instagram while out with friends, which keeps me apart from them</td></tr><tr><th>Empty filler<div class="small">Screens fail to fill the time or the gap (negative)</div></th><td>Watching, scrolling or gaming to pass time when alone becomes boring, leaves the speaker still lonely, or brings their mood down.</td><td>TV too boring at night, YouTube that does not take away being alone, phone and telly getting boring indoors, the explore page bringing mood down.</td><td>Watching or playing that does lift the mood (goes to Entertainment)</td><td>telly and phone only fill a rainy day indoors for so long before they get boring and I get agitated, watching YouTube alone in my room doesn&#x27;t take away feeling alone, video games bore me after ten minutes, so gaming sessions with friends leave me unsatisfied, browsing the Instagram explore page to pass time brings my mood down, watching a Netflix series no one else watches leaves me lonely with no one to talk to about it, TV is too boring to take my mind off lonely thoughts at night, at home TV can be boring and doesn&#x27;t make up for having no one to talk to</td></tr><tr><th>Cut off without a phone<div class="small">Being without a phone cuts me off (negative)</div></th><td>Not having a phone leaves the speaker feeling lost, left out, angry or stressed, because so much contact runs through it.</td><td>Times without a phone.</td><td>Choosing to log off (goes to On my own terms)</td><td>being without a phone makes me feel lost because so much communication happens through it on Snapchat and Instagram, being without a phone makes me feel angry, not having my phone makes me feel left out, like there is a lot I am missing out on, without my phone I feel stressed and annoyed at having to find another way to reach friends instead of just calling them</td></tr><tr><th>Fakery<div class="small">Dismay at social media&#x27;s fakery (negative)</div></th><td>The speaker&#x27;s own sadness, despair or disappointment at the false personas and hype of social media.</td><td>Despair that it will never change; being let down when polished profiles meet reality.</td><td>Feeling inferior to what is shown (goes to Comparison)</td><td>getting worked up over people&#x27;s polished profiles, then being let down when I meet them in person, thinking about how social media has got out of control leaves me sad and hopeless, the fake personas social platforms push people into give me a feeling of despair because it will never change</td></tr><tr><th>Keeping in touch<div class="small">Reaching and keeping in touch with people (positive)</div></th><td>Calls, messages and social media connect the speaker with friends and family, or with someone to share something with, easing loneliness or lifting them.</td><td>Calling a friend when alone, family texts during exams, staying connected to friends who moved away, a shared series to talk about.</td><td>Valued mainly for the control it gives over when and how to be in contact (goes to On my own terms)</td><td>having my phone and laptop at home makes me feel more socially connected through social media, social media keeps me connected to everyone, including friends going off to university, quick text messages from family checking on me during exams, knowing I can call a friend whenever I want company makes being alone at home feel alright, calling someone is how I end the boredom of my own company, messaging or calling someone when bored at home gets me together with them so I am not alone, phoning family when living alone helped a little with the loneliness and overthinking, being on the phone with a friend makes being alone in my apartment less bad, close friends just a phone call away lit me up when I felt down or lonely, sharing a Netflix series with someone gives us something to talk about and fills the loneliness</td></tr><tr><th>On my own terms<div class="small">Contact on my own terms (positive)</div></th><td>Technology lets the speaker connect while keeping control: choosing when to talk, logging off, avoiding faces and looks, curating whom they follow.</td><td>Being herself from bed with her phone, logging off when it gets too much, managing her feed.</td><td>Contact valued simply for reaching someone (goes to Keeping in touch)</td><td>because I manage who I follow and talk to, being on social media normally feels good, talking to people on the phone on my own terms lets me be myself without waiting to be accepted, being in bed with my phone I can be myself and still feel accepted and socially connected, being able to log off lets me escape when socialising gets too much, chatting on social networks spares me the faces and looks that make me feel judged, communicating on Snapchat/Facebook/Instagram when I choose makes me feel socially connected</td></tr><tr><th>Entertainment<div class="small">Entertainment and distraction (positive)</div></th><td>Watching, scrolling, gaming or music entertains the speaker, passes time pleasantly or lifts their mood.</td><td>Social media making them laugh, YouTube in alone time, Netflix, console games, upbeat music.</td><td>Music or videos turned to as comfort in loneliness or to feel understood (goes to Comfort)</td><td>social media makes me laugh; I like it, I use social media as an entertainment tool, listening to hip-hop gives me an upbeat tempo and changes my attitude, putting music on in the morning makes me feel better, and the effect lasts after it stops, going on my phone and watching telly is a way I like to chill indoors, inspiring and funny things people post on social media make me laugh and feel better about myself, listening to music keeps me entertained and makes being alone fun, watching Netflix gives me something to do so being alone in my apartment is less bad, watching YouTube in my alone time is something I enjoy and that makes good alone time, playing on a console helps me forget loneliness and negative thoughts</td></tr><tr><th>Comfort<div class="small">Comfort and feeling understood (positive)</div></th><td>When low or lonely, the speaker turns to music or videos for comfort, to feel understood or to keep going.</td><td>Artists they relate to, music that shares the feeling, motivational videos.</td><td>Plain enjoyment with no low mood being met (goes to Entertainment)</td><td>listening to music alone when lonely gives me a sense of comfort and helps me escape my state, turning to artists I relate to when lonely lets me share the loneliness and understand my emotion better, understanding and relating to UK rap lyrics is the best feeling in the world, music lets me share a feeling with someone else, so the loneliness goes, watching motivational YouTube videos helps keep me going when lonely</td></tr><tr><th>Recognition and motivation<div class="small">Recognition and motivation (positive)</div></th><td>Posting brings recognition, or images spur the speaker on, which makes them feel better about themselves.</td><td>Posting stories with friends, gym pictures that motivate.</td><td>The same images when they overwhelm (goes to Comparison)</td><td>posting Snapchat stories of me out with friends for others to see makes me feel better inside, through recognition, pictures of muscular people on apps motivate me to go to the gym and better myself</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Left in no item: reading about my symptoms on the internet scared me all the time (Health anxiety from reading about symptoms online: one account, about information rather than contact, entertainment or self-image, and fits no role.).</p><details><summary>What the grouping merged, split or left alone</summary><p>Each item keeps one side of mood, since the question keeps positive and negative apart. Music was kept apart as comfort where the speaker turns to it when low, and as entertainment where it simply lifts the mood. Seeing that one is left out was kept apart from general comparison, because the first is about a particular event the speaker missed and the second about their standing against others. Screens coming between people in company was kept apart from screens failing to fill time alone. One label, a health scare from reading online, fits no role and is left unplaced.</p></details></section>
<section class="rb-block" id="s-howdone-t-any" data-node="step:t_any"><h4>Counting: Any</h4><p class="small">People by mood</p><p class="table-said">People who tie technology to a positive, and to a negative, feeling of their own at least once, out of the 12 interviewed.</p><div class="rb-block" data-node="asset:t_any"><details><summary>The table <span class="small">3 cells</span></summary><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Mood</th><th>People</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Negative</td><td>10 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Positive</td><td>10 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Mixed</td><td>0 of 12</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details></div></section>
<section class="rb-block" id="s-howdone-t-role" data-node="step:t_role"><h4>Counting: Role</h4><p class="small">People by item</p><p class="table-said">People who describe each role technology plays in their own mood, out of the 12 interviewed.</p><div class="rb-block" data-node="asset:t_role"><details><summary>The table <span class="small">15 cells</span></summary><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>People</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Entertainment</td><td>6 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Keeping in touch</td><td>6 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Comparison</td><td>5 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Empty filler</td><td>5 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Hiding</td><td>3 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Comfort</td><td>2 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Fakery</td><td>2 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Left out</td><td>2 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Let down by messages</td><td>2 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>On my own terms</td><td>2 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Screens between people</td><td>2 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Cut off without a phone</td><td>1 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Recognition and motivation</td><td>1 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>None</td><td>1 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>OTHER</td><td>0 of 12</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details></div></section>
<section class="rb-block" id="s-howdone-t-role-unprompted" data-node="step:t_role_unprompted"><h4>Counting: Role unprompted</h4><p class="small">People by item</p><p class="table-said">People who describe each role at least once without the interviewer having raised technology, out of the 12 interviewed.</p><div class="rb-block" data-node="asset:t_role_unprompted"><details><summary>The table <span class="small">15 cells</span></summary><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>People</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Keeping in touch</td><td>6 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Comparison</td><td>4 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Empty filler</td><td>4 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Entertainment</td><td>4 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Hiding</td><td>2 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Left out</td><td>2 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Let down by messages</td><td>2 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Screens between people</td><td>2 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Comfort</td><td>1 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Cut off without a phone</td><td>1 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>On my own terms</td><td>1 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Recognition and motivation</td><td>1 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>None</td><td>1 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Fakery</td><td>0 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>OTHER</td><td>0 of 12</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details></div></section>
<section class="rb-block" id="s-howdone-t-medium" data-node="step:t_medium"><h4>Counting: Medium</h4><p class="small">People by medium and mood</p><p class="table-said">People who tie each kind of technology to a positive or a negative feeling of their own, out of the 12 interviewed.</p><div class="rb-block" data-node="asset:t_medium"><details><summary>The table <span class="small">12 cells</span></summary><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Medium</th><th>Mood</th><th>People</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Social media</td><td>Negative</td><td>7 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Messaging calls</td><td>Positive</td><td>6 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Gaming streaming</td><td>Negative</td><td>5 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Gaming streaming</td><td>Positive</td><td>5 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Messaging calls</td><td>Negative</td><td>4 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Other tech</td><td>Negative</td><td>4 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Social media</td><td>Positive</td><td>4 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Other tech</td><td>Positive</td><td>2 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Gaming streaming</td><td>Mixed</td><td>0 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Messaging calls</td><td>Mixed</td><td>0 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Other tech</td><td>Mixed</td><td>0 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Social media</td><td>Mixed</td><td>0 of 12</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details></div></section>
<section class="rb-block" id="s-howdone-t-off" data-node="step:t_off"><h4>Counting: Off</h4><p class="small">People by kind</p><p class="table-said">People whose interview holds each kind of material that bears on the question but falls outside its frame, out of the 12 interviewed.</p><div class="rb-block" data-node="asset:t_off"><details><summary>The table <span class="small">4 cells</span></summary><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>People</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>General view</td><td>9 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Doubt</td><td>6 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Rejected suggestion</td><td>2 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Other effect</td><td>1 of 12</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details></div></section>
<section class="rb-block" id="s-howdone-p-sides" data-node="step:p_sides"><h4>Profiling: Sides</h4></section>
<section class="rb-block" id="s-howdone-t-sides" data-node="step:t_sides"><h4>Counting: Sides</h4><p class="small">People by any positive and any negative</p><p class="table-said">People by whether they tie technology to a positive feeling of their own, a negative one, both or neither, out of the 12 interviewed.</p><div class="rb-block" data-node="asset:t_sides"><details><summary>The table <span class="small">4 cells</span></summary><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Any positive</th><th>Any negative</th><th>People</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>9 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>1 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>1 of 12</td></tr><tr><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>1 of 12</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details></div></section>
<section class="rb-block" id="s-howdone-k" data-node="step:k"><h4>Tagging claims: K</h4><ul><li>Contact with a particular person through a call, a message or a shared series is told mostly as easing loneliness, while watching or playing alone is told both ways: as failing to fill the gap and as filling time or lifting mood.<br><span class="small">Calls, messages and a shared series ease being alone in I17, I24, I28, I29 and I43; screens used alone leave people still alone or bored in I17, I28, I43 and I48 but lift or fill time in I17, I28 and I42; against the first half, I04 and I42 describe messages and calls that hurt. Rests on: I28, I29, I17, I28, I24, I24, I43, I28, I48, I48, I43, I17, I28, I42, I42, I17. Against: I42, I42, I42, I04. Found by: Rows whose item is Keeping in touch, Empty filler or Entertainment, read for whether another person is reached through the screen; then rows whose item is Let down by messages, for contact that hurts.</span></li><li>Most of the negative accounts of social media are about the measure it holds up: others&#x27; better lives, looks or luxury, and events the speaker was left out of.<br><span class="small">Five of the seven people who tie social media to a negative feeling (I06, I07, I17, I42, I43) describe seeing others look happier, richer or fitter, or together without them; the other negative social media accounts are fear of being judged (I28), dismay at its fakery (I04, I42), a false online character (I42) and browsing that brings mood down or keeps the speaker apart from friends (I43). Rests on: I06, I06, I07, I07, I07, I17, I17, I43, I43, I06, I06, I06, I06, I06, I42. Against: I04, I28, I28, I28, I42, I42, I42, I43, I43. Found by: Rows with medium social_media and mood negative, read for what the feed shows the speaker.</span></li><li>In I37 the control a phone gives over contact is told both as a relief and as a means of making excuses that keep them away from people.<br><span class="small">I37 values being able to log off and talk from bed, and also texts excuses to stay in bed; I07 manages whom they follow so that social media normally feels good; against this, I24 describes being without a phone as loss, not relief. Rests on: I37, I37, I37, I37, I37, I37, I07. Against: I24, I24. Found by: Rows whose item is On my own terms, and the Hiding row in the same interview.</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="rb-block" id="s-howdone-answer" data-node="step:answer"><h4>Writing: Answer</h4><details><summary>The instruction the answer was written to</summary><blockquote></blockquote></details></section>
<h4>What the evaluator was asked to agree</h4><p><b>the 12 pilot interviews</b> (2026-10-20T15:02:00+01:00). Put to the evaluator in question cards their app showed them; their answer is as the AI that asked recorded it. They answered: Use the 12-interview pilot sample (I04, I06, I07, I17, I18, I24, I28, I29, I37, I42, I43, I48), drawn at random within borough and gender? <i>I agree, the question needs tweaking</i>.</p><ul class="small"><li>The documents, as shown under What was read.</li></ul><p class="small">Each was set out to them only by the AI that asked, so that it was shown is the AI's word.</p><p><b>the settled question on technology and mood</b> (2026-10-20T15:02:00+01:00). Put to the evaluator in question cards their app showed them; their answer is as the AI that asked recorded it. They answered: Does technology and social media replace the upbringing question, or join it? <i>Replace it</i>; By &#x27;maintenance&#x27;, do you mean what keeps a negative mood going once it is there, as opposed to what starts it? <i>Any role at all (of positive and negative mood)</i>; For technology, describe or judge? <i>Describe roles, no verdict (Recommended)</i>; Go with this settled question? <i>Go (Recommended)</i>; General remarks about social media that the speaker doesn&#x27;t apply to themselves: <i>Leave out (Recommended)</i>; Add a short section for anything that bears on the question but falls outside this frame? <i>Yes</i>. They asked for this change: let&#x27;s look at technology / social media and its different roles in maintenance ... of pos/neg mood.</p><ul class="small"><li>The question, as shown at the head of this report.</li></ul><p class="small">Each was set out to them only by the AI that asked, so that it was shown is the AI's word.</p><p><b>the codebook of 13 roles</b> (2026-10-20T15:14:22+01:00). Put to the evaluator in question cards their app showed them; their answer is as the AI that asked recorded it. They answered: Use these 13 roles to count by? <i>Use them (Recommended)</i>; Roles that rest on one person (Recognition and motivation; Comfort is mostly I06): <i>Keep them apart (Recommended)</i>.</p><ul class="small"><li>Grouping: Roles, as this report shows it.</li></ul><p class="small">Each was set out to them only by the AI that asked, so that it was shown is the AI's word.</p>
<h4>The checks that were run</h4><ul><li>100 of 100 coded passages found word for word in their document.</li><li>48 of 48 quotations in the answer found word for word in the documents (a quotation of under twelve characters, such as one word, is not checked).</li><li>44 numbers in the answer, every one computed directly from the coded passages rather than written by the AI; 0 written by the AI without a count behind them.</li><li>0 citations to passages that do not exist; 0 quotations not in the passage they cite.</li></ul>

<section class="rb-block" id="s-howdone-check" data-node="step:check"><h4>The check</h4><p>A separate reader read the answer against the passages it cites and the documents, and checked every sentence and quotation of the answer against the documents, correcting the coding and the answer where they were wrong. The answer above is the corrected one, recounted after the check.</p><ul><li>Disagreements ruled: none. No second coder coded these documents, so there is no recode/disagreements.md: 0 ruled for the rows, 0 for the second coder, 0 unsettled.</li><li>Rows changed: 1 c_tech row removed; 6 c_off rows added; 1 c_off point reworded (quotation unchanged); 1 label taken out of the Empty filler item in judged/g_roles.json (with its &quot;counts&quot; example); 3 claim readings in judged/k.json corrected (connect, measure, control).</li><li>Sentences changed: 29 changes below, covering about 40 sentences, plus the role list added at the top. All 12 documents were read in full.</li><li>Recount: no quotation found in no document, none outside what it cites, no id naming no cell; audit warnings weighed below.</li></ul></section>
</div>
<h3>The record</h3><p>This is run <code>2026-10-20-wxbh</code>. Its ID stays the same through every revision, and the zip's fingerprint, below, names this version of it. This report was created directly from the run's zip, in-the-12-pilot-interviews-i04-i06-i07-i17-i18-i24-2026-10-20-9d3132de.zip, with Rubicon 0.1.2+b336babacc8f. The zip's fingerprint is <code class="fingerprint">9d3132dedddec1cb5b7d536904c17c7896ff28efc20cabb6daa7afcfd599e98a</code>. It is the SHA-256 of the zip's SHA256SUMS file, which lists the SHA-256 of every other file in the zip, so changing any file breaks the match. To check that the zip is the one this report was created from, unzip it, run <code>sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS</code>, then <code>sha256sum SHA256SUMS</code>, and compare the result with the fingerprint above. Rubicon's own code checks the rest: <code>python verify.py &lt;report&gt; &lt;zip&gt;</code> recounts every number from the coded passages in the zip, creates the report again from the zip and says whether it matches this one exactly.</p><h2>Passages cited</h2><p class="note">I28: "Um well, I wouldn’t say, I would say the most lonely place would be my apartment just because when I’m in my apartment I’m so on my own but even then you know, it’s not as bad as living in Essex, because even when I’m in my apartment I have some things to do, like whether I’m on the phone with a friend or I’m watching you know, Netflix on the laptop so I’d say the most loneliest would be my apartment."</p><p class="note">I28: "Yes, it’s not physical interaction but then, even if you’re calling, it w- it should minimise the effect. And, you know, I started doing that- it did help a little bit, not too much."</p><p class="note">I29: "Whereas- like for example, once I left university and sixth form and all sorts, you usually stay in contact with all those friends ‘cause they were the ones that made you feel special, they’re the ones that lit- you know, lit you up when you felt so down or lonely, just a phone call away."</p><p class="note">I24: "Like me, I will enjoy my own company for a certain amount of time, but after a while I’ll get bored of it and I’ll think ‘kay let me go call someone and see what they’re doing because I’m bored of being here now."</p><p class="note">I17: "Alright, I feel like. ‘Cause if I know that I want to be by myself, then I know I’ll be alright when I want to be by myself. I know that if I want to be with one of my friends, I could call one of my friends."</p><p class="note">I07: "like during exam season like my family members were checking up on me, just quick text messages here and there like wishing me well and stuff like that"</p><p class="note">I42: "I like going on YouTube and just watching stuff that I never think to learn, that type thing. There’s stuff on YouTube that’s great, and then there’s dumb stuff on YouTube, it’s like so bad stuff but it’s that, it’s, it’s that alone time that allows you to do that type of stuff, that allows you to imagine yourself in different situations."</p><p class="note">I17: "Because sometimes I do like to chill indoors and I do like to sit there and go on my phone and watch telly, but I do also like to go out and go do things with my mates and interact with other people, which is good."</p><p class="note">I06: "so I think that for me if I feel lonely or am I at a down point I will list-, turn to the artist I can relate to, so we can kind of share this, well for me, I can share this loneliness with them and then understand my emotion better"</p><p class="note">I06: "Yeah, and I feel like that&#x27;s why music helps as well, because you can share that, that kind of feeling with someone else, um I feel like once you can relate to someone then the loneliness goes, the sense of loneliness goes."</p><p class="note">I28: "With everything [unintelligible 32:58] internet, you know, go on YouTube, watch motivational videos, like you know, whatever helps."</p><p class="note">I37: "It could be anywhere but as long as i can be comfortable so its like my partner that I&#x27;m comfortable with and in work, uhm, being in bed with my phone it&#x27;s like I can be myself and still be accepted."</p><p class="note">I37: "So if i feel like i&#x27;m too far into a situation where I can&#x27;t get out and i feel like i just can&#x27;t do it. I can just log off, you know. When you&#x27;re with people you cannot just walk away."</p><p class="note">I07: "but generally speaking I&#x27;m quite careful with how I manage my social media, who I follow, who I talk to, so it&#x27;s not an unpleasant feeling when I&#x27;m on social media, it&#x27;s normally good."</p><p class="note">I06: "So that kind of, that kind of snap of me, maybe with my friends, makes me feel better inside in comparison to someone that doesn&#x27;t and it&#x27;s mo-, it&#x27;s more about recognition from o-, again is recognition from other people now thinking about it"</p><p class="note">I06: "and I think with certain apps you can, you can challenge yourself in a positive way. So, um sometimes when you see a picture of a kind of muscular man or muscular women, you kind of feel the need that ‘Ah I should go to the gym’ or like do activities that just make me better."</p><p class="note">I17: "Well if you’re sad and you’re lonely and you’ve got no one to talk to, then I feel like it makes you feel kind of worse. Not worse, but it does a little bit, because you’re like… you’re sad, you’re lonely, you’ve got nothing to do, you just feel like everything’s going so wrong, but when you look at them and they’re all happy and their living this perfect- like it literally looks like this perfect little life that they’re living, but it’s not really a perfect life, if you know what I mean?"</p><p class="note">I07: "it will be like a subconscious thought that I just have to repress because like you just see something and cause you ain’t got it, you just, I just almighty feel like they&#x27;re better than me or I&#x27;m not, I&#x27;m not, I&#x27;m not pretty enough to be accepted or I&#x27;m not this, I’m not that, so, and obviously those thoughts there can just spark feelings of just like being upset and angry, just a bit like, just a bit hope, like a bit of hopelessness like, ok like, yeah, I haven&#x27;t got that so what’s the point, yeah."</p><p class="note">I43: "So, I feel like everything I see on Instagram and Facebook, I, sometimes does get a bit jealousy, but at the end of the day you never know what&#x27;s really happening behind the scenes of that person&#x27;s life."</p><p class="note">I37: "It could be getting up in the morning, like i said, straight away you penalise yourself because you don’t look like the models on TV when they wake up but we all know that they haven’t been asleep you know so it’s like everything becomes a struggle because- it’s like I dont know so- just doing your hair in the morning is a struggle because you don’t want to do it but  you have to do it because that’s what’s expected."</p><p class="note">I06: "so when you… when you see pictures that you&#x27;re not involved in and it just generally seems fun, that&#x27;s when you start feeling alone because you&#x27;re not… with them, you’re not going to be in the background smiling or anything, it’s more you sitting down on your bed or something and you&#x27;re looking, tapping, seeing that loads of photos… you can’t, can’t really escape these kinda um photos and these situations, where you&#x27;re gonna bound to be and where you&#x27;re not involved in."</p><p class="note">I42: "I noticed on a, just a, like, Facebook, can’t remember it was Facebook but I noticed that a few of the lads went out and I asked them: ‘you went out?’ and they were like ‘oh sorry, didn’t think you wanted to come’, and then I basically replied with ‘it’s fine, I was busy’, which was a lie but it was one of those “do not show face that you wanted to be there even though…”."</p><p class="note">I48: "So, as long as I’m lonely, I’m going to have to continue thinking about stuff. Sometimes, TV can be boring to watch, so you just turn it off, yeah, try to sleep but."</p><p class="note">I28: "It’s literally just me in the room, know I’d try going on the internet, you know maybe uh… do something on the internet like uh I tend to watch a lot of YouTube videos. But at the end of the day, you know, I’m still alone."</p><p class="note">I28: "It- if- in a way, it make me feel like, you know, I’m forced to sort of keep myself to myself. ‘Cause of the way sort of everyone is."</p><p class="note">I28: "And they would want an excuse, and often [unintelligible 29:55] uh the real reason that I don’t have it is because um… I just felt like, you know, I don’t want to be judged so it’s best not to have these things."</p><p class="note">I37: "So final box is just making excuses so I’ve put like a message image, so like- want to come over- no sorry I can’t- why- I’m very busy. But the reality is you’re in bed. You don’t want to say- oh no I don’t want to because I don’t want to. Because you’re scared of their opinion so you find any opportunity to find an excuse."</p><p class="note">I42: "Loneliness is in, I, when I was, when I, I used to have thoughts like this all the time and I used to, I used to sit there and think, you have to mentally steady yourself to use social media outlets, stuff like that."</p><p class="note">I04: "and then your phone goes off and you look at the phone to see if it&#x27;s that person and it&#x27;s not that person. So, I don&#x27;t know how you describe that feeling, maybe anticipation, anticipating that they text you, even though you&#x27;ve broken up with them and you know, they&#x27;re not going to text you, because you don&#x27;t want to seem like the weak to count upon to text them."</p><p class="note">I42: "I kind of thought was the whole sitting at home, looking at your phone, waiting for an invitation or something like that and it&#x27;s just doesn&#x27;t come through type of thing, and it just experiences of it, happening, it does make you feel lonely."</p><p class="note">I42: "I remembered going and seeing a few friends and then I remembered messaging them and they’re like ‘no, we’re gonna stay in for the night’ type thing and that really did affect my, whether I was lonely or not, because I was like, they just lied to me cause I know I saw them it was, it’s, it’s a weird, it’s a weird feeling to be lied to for something like that,"</p><p class="note">I42: "it was like people were just calling and texting just to check up, to make sure I was fine, calling a family member to ask about me type of thing and it kinda of got depressing for the fact of they were scared if they came to me and said if I was feeling, if I was feeling fine or stuff like that, that I’d actually push away more than I had already had been type of thing."</p><p class="note">I43: "Sometimes it does get annoying in terms of like, right, all of us came out, we’re sitting in the restaurant eating and just, just talk all of us. Even myself I do that because everyone else is on it and like, it kind of takes that connection away because at the end of the day you just wanna go in."</p><p class="note">I42: "Okay, so two friends are playing football on the TV, and they’re like fifa, and obviously you can play four players but...I don’t like fifa, it’s the worst game ever, so, so it’s just something I don’t like, so even though my friends like it and you feel like you have to put up that type of, you have to put up with that, just, just having something that you don’t like and they do like, you kind of have to disconnect with people."</p><p class="note">I42: "So yeah, I don’t think, I think, it’s just the feeling that it gives me is one of despair because it’s not gonna change, it’s never gonna change, like, we gonna get rid of Facebook?"</p><p class="note">I24: "But this links a lot to like loneliness because many people like- even me, like when I haven’t had a phone, sometimes I feel so lost because… a lot of communication happens through it on so- on social media. Like Snapchat, Instagram and stuff."</p><p class="note">I28: "And then I started reading some stuff on the internet and it- it- you know, internet starts- it wasn’t the type of- like people- I was reading, you know, people would start doing that, they would go crazy, they would commit suicide, and that was just scaring me like all the time."</p><p class="note">I43: "Because sometimes you might watch a Netflix series and no one watches it. Sometimes you feel lonely in the sense of like you have no one to talk to because when you watch a really good episode you kind of want to get it out because you want to hear that another person&#x27;s opinion"</p><p class="note">I43: "you start seeing the person that watches the same series, you can talk with each other and it kind of fills that loneliness."</p><p class="note">I17: "Like, you can watch telly, you can go on your phone, but that’s literally gonna get boring soon, do you know what I mean? There’s only so much telly you can watch, there’s only so much you can be on your phone before you start getting distracted, and then you’re like sitting there, and then you get agitated because you’re like, what can I do, where can I go, and you look at the weather and it’s just like, ugh."</p><p class="note">I48: "I put home, because [laughing], there isn’t anyone to talk to, and TV can be boring at times, so."</p><p class="note">I28: "Um well, I wouldn’t say, I would say the most lonely place would be my apartment just because when I’m in my apartment I’m so on my own but even then you know, it’s not as bad as living in Essex, because even when I’m in my apartment I have some things to do, like whether I’m on the phone with a friend or I’m watching you know, Netflix on the laptop so I’d say the most loneliest would be my apartment."</p><p class="note">I42: "your feeling of loneliness can be sated in multiple different ways like, for example, reading a book can take your loneliness away because you’re, you’re working to forget about it in your own different way, like, going on, going on a console to play a game, you’re forgetting about things, those type of negative things"</p><p class="note">I24: "It’s like people get addicted to phones sometimes, so when you’re not having it, it’s like you feel there’s a lot that you’re missing out on."</p><p class="note">I29: "‘Cause you’re looking into a person, due to their looks or, you know, what they have in life such as, you know, um… luxury stuff, that’s not very like pe- permanent for you. So normally people look at that, and that leads them to be lonely."</p><p class="note">I04: "I think social media is just like drugs *laughs* for some people, so yeah, some people get hooked on it, some people can do it once and just leave it."</p><p class="note">I17: "Um… Mm, I don’t really, I don’t really feel anything about it, I don’t know. Like, it’s like… I dunno? It’s like, kind of like, fake?"</p><p class="note">I17: "Well, I don’t do it personally, but other people, I feel like it could be quite sad."</p><p class="note">I06: "but I felt the need to um for others to see and compare themselves with to make myself feel better."</p><p class="note">I42: "I’m more of an introvert so I take it as a good thing when I don’t get invited to things so it’s a lot harder for me to kind of feel loneliness cause it’s just something I’m kind of happy with"</p><p class="note">I07: "so, there&#x27;s always that feeling of ‘Oh, I want that’, as opposed to ‘Oh’, like it was never, it&#x27;s never a negative feeling"</p><p class="note">I24: "Makes me feel unwanted. Um… like it makes you question yourself. Um… like, does anyone like me? But not in this case."</p><p class="note">I28: "But what I used to tell them was like oh, you know, I don’t wanna get distracted in my uni. That’s why I don’t have it."</p><p class="note">I42: "Um, it wasn’t more a feeling left out, it was just, it wasn’t mentally sating me if you know what I mean?"</p><p class="note">I17: "Yeah, with that and with other things, kind of thing, do you know what I mean? It’s not just a phone situation, just things in general life, do you know what I mean?"</p><p class="note">I04: "I missed 45 minutes of it, I was like ‘that really 45 minutes?’ so I rewind it, watched it all the way back, next minute is quarter to 1:00 in the morning, so a massive time-waster."</p><h2>What next</h2><p><b>For the evaluator:</b> Treat this report as a draft. Before it is final, make sense of it with the people it concerns, such as programme staff and participants: whether the findings ring true, what they leave out, and what to do about them. You can then use the Rubicon plugin to work their responses back into a revised answer.</p><p><b>For evaluation commissioners:</b> Parallel to this report is a zip file (in-the-12-pilot-interviews-i04-i06-i07-i17-i18-i24-2026-10-20-9d3132de.zip), saved in the same folder, which contains the whole run: the documents, the coded passages and the workflow that recounts every number. Its fingerprint, under How this report was written, ties it to this report: change a file in either and they no longer match. With it, anyone can check every number against the coded passages, and code the documents again to the same definitions without Rubicon or any other particular software. A fresh coding will not reproduce this report word for word, because AI models are unpredictable, but it should come close.</p><p>The Rubicon plugin for Claude is provided for free by Causal Map Ltd. Causal Map also runs workshops and consultancy on analysing qualitative evidence for evaluation: <a href="https://causalmap.app/contact/?utm_source=rubicon-plugin&amp;utm_medium=report">get in touch</a>, or follow <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/causalmap/">Causal Map on LinkedIn</a>. Coming soon: continue your Rubicon work at the Rubicon website.</p><p class="carbon"><b><span class="flame">🔥</span> Carbon cost of this report: Between 0.039 and 7.2 km driven in an average UK family car</b></p><div class="carbon-more"><p>Making this report used between 51.9 and 9586 watt-hours of electricity, which is the carbon of driving an average UK family car for that distance. A ballpark only: Anthropic publishes no energy figures, and the run, made in the evaluator's own Claude, records no token counts, so the estimate is made from the run's size. The 48 documents were read in full once by each of the 2 coding steps, by the second coder and by the check, and the writer read the tables; everything the run wrote counts as output. That comes to about 2,435,246 tokens read and 45,444 written, at 4 characters a token.</p><ul><li>High: 0.39 Wh per thousand tokens read and 1.95 per thousand written, with what was read counted 10 times over for the agent's own overhead. Simon P. Couch, &#x27;Electricity use of AI coding agents&#x27;, 20 January 2026, https://simonpcouch.com/blog/2026-01-20-cc-impact/: about 390 Wh per million input tokens and 1,950 per million output tokens for Claude Opus, back-solved from Epoch AI&#x27;s estimate for a 100,000-token prompt with output at five times the input rate, as Anthropic&#x27;s prices are. The author calls them napkin maths. Our assumption, not a measurement: an agent re-sends its instructions, earlier turns and tool output with every request, so it reads far more than the documents themselves. The low case counts only what was read and written; the high case counts what was read ten times over.</li><li>Low: the same rates divided by 20, what was read counted once. Oviedo et al., &#x27;Energy use of AI inference, efficiency pathways, and test-time scaling&#x27;, arXiv:2509.20241: a median 0.31 Wh per query for frontier models, and widely cited estimates overstated by 4 to 20 times. The low case takes the high case&#x27;s rates divided by the top of that range.</li><li>0.124 kg CO2e per kWh. DESNZ greenhouse gas reporting conversion factors, UK grid electricity, 2024.</li><li>0.16601 kg CO2e per km. DESNZ greenhouse gas reporting conversion factors, average car of unknown fuel, 2024.</li></ul></div><p class="madewith">Made with Rubicon, free for Claude: <a class="site" href="https://rubicon.causalmap.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener">rubicon.causalmap.app</a></p></body></html>